  {'num': '9.1', 'short': 'Blood pressure, thin air, pressure as a scalar', 'scene': None, 'note': EXPL,
   'question': 'Explain why (a) The blood pressure in humans is greater at the feet than at the brain (b) Atmospheric pressure at a height of about 6 km decreases to nearly half of its value at the sea level, though the height of the atmosphere is more than 100 km (c) Hydrostatic pressure is a scalar quantity even though pressure is force divided by area.',
   'given': ['Three observations.'], 'asked': 'Explanations.',
   'answer': ['(a) Pressure in a fluid increases with depth (P = P₀ + ρgh). The column of blood above the feet is about 1.5 m taller than above the brain, so the pressure there is higher.',
              '(b) Air is compressible: its density falls rapidly with height, so most of the air (and its weight) lies in the lowest few kilometres.',
              '(c) At a point in a fluid the pressure is the same in every direction; it has no particular direction, so it is a scalar. The force on a surface is perpendicular to that surface whichever way it faces.'],
   'final': '(a) greater depth of blood (b) air density falls with height (c) pressure acts equally in all directions.'},
  {'num': '9.2', 'short': 'Contact angles, wetting, drops', 'scene': None, 'note': EXPL,
   'question': 'Explain why (a) The angle of contact of mercury with glass is obtuse, while that of water with glass is acute. (b) Water on a clean glass surface tends to spread out while mercury on the same surface tends to form drops. (Put differently, water wets glass while mercury does not.) (c) Surface tension of a liquid is independent of the area of the surface (d) Water with detergent disolved in it should have small angles of contact. (e) A drop of liquid under no external forces is always spherical in shape',
   'given': ['Five observations about surfaces of liquids.'], 'asked': 'Explanations.',
   'answer': ['(a) For mercury the cohesive forces (Hg–Hg) are stronger than the adhesive forces (Hg–glass): obtuse angle. For water, adhesion to glass beats cohesion: acute angle.',
              '(b) Same reason: water is attracted more to glass than to itself and spreads; mercury pulls itself together into drops.',
              '(c) Surface tension is a force per unit length (or energy per unit area) of the surface: a property of the liquid and its surroundings, not of how much surface there is.',
              '(d) Detergent lowers the surface tension of water against air (and against greasy surfaces), so the water spreads and wets: a small angle of contact. That is what lets it get into the fibres of cloth.',
              '(e) With no other forces, surface tension makes the surface as small as possible; for a given volume the sphere has the least area.'],
   'final': 'Cohesion vs adhesion; surface tension is a per-length property; detergents reduce it; a sphere has the least area.'},
  {'num': '9.3', 'short': 'Fill in the blanks: fluids', 'scene': None, 'note': EXPL,
   'question': 'Fill in the blanks using the word(s) from the list appended with each statement: (a) Surface tension of liquids generally . . . with temperatures (increases / decreases) (b) Viscosity of gases . . . with temperature, whereas viscosity of liquids . . . with temperature (increases / decreases) (c) For solids with elastic modulus of rigidity, the shearing force is proportional to . . . , while for fluids it is proportional to . . . (shear strain / rate of shear strain) (d) For a fluid in a steady flow, the increase in flow speed at a constriction follows (conservation of mass / Bernoulli’s principle) (e) For the model of a plane in a wind tunnel, turbulence occurs at a ... speed for turbulence for an actual plane (greater / smaller)',
   'given': ['Five statements with blanks.'], 'asked': 'The correct words.',
   'answer': ['(a) decreases', '(b) increases; decreases', '(c) shear strain; rate of shear strain', '(d) conservation of mass (the equation of continuity)', '(e) greater: turbulence sets in at a fixed Reynolds number ρvL/η; the model is smaller (smaller L), so it needs a greater speed.'],
   'final': '(a) decreases (b) increases, decreases (c) shear strain, rate of shear strain (d) conservation of mass (e) greater'},
  {'num': '9.4', 'short': 'Bernoulli in everyday life', 'scene': None, 'note': EXPL,
   'question': 'Explain why (a) To keep a piece of paper horizontal, you should blow over, not under, it (b) When we try to close a water tap with our fingers, fast jets of water gush through the openings between our fingers (c) The size of the needle of a syringe controls flow rate better than the thumb pressure exerted by a doctor while administering an injection (d) A fluid flowing out of a small hole in a vessel results in a backward thrust on the vessel (e) A spinning cricket ball in air does not follow a parabolic trajectory',
   'given': ['Five observations.'], 'asked': 'Explanations.',
   'answer': ['(a) Blowing over the paper makes the air above move fast, lowering its pressure (Bernoulli); the higher pressure below holds the paper up.',
              '(b) Continuity: the same flow through a much smaller opening must move much faster (Av = constant).',
              '(c) Speed of flow depends strongly on the opening (and by Poiseuille, flow rate ∝ r<sup>4</sup>); the needle’s small size sets the flow, while thumb pressure changes it only a little (v ∝ √ΔP in Bernoulli).',
              '(d) The fluid gains momentum as it rushes out through the hole; by Newton’s third law the vessel gets equal momentum backwards.',
              '(e) Spin drags air round the ball, making it move faster on one side than the other; the pressure difference (Magnus effect) gives a sideways force, so the path bends away from a parabola.'],
   'final': 'Bernoulli, continuity, Poiseuille, momentum conservation, Magnus effect.'},
